"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium bg-violet-100 text-violet-800 dark:bg-violet-500/20 dark:text-violet-400 border-violet-300 dark:border-violet-500/30\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"State Tax\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"June 19, 2026\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"h3\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Texas Tax Guide 2026: No Income Tax, But What About Property and Sales Tax?\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Texas has no state income tax, but property tax is among the highest. Complete guide to Texas taxes in 2026.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"texas tax calculator\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"texas tax calculator\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"income tax in texas\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"income tax in texas\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"taxable income\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"taxable income\\\"}]]}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"washington-tax-guide-2026\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog/washington-tax-guide-2026\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card p-5 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$L2a\\\",\\\"$L2b\\\",\\\"$L2c\\\",\\\"$L2d\\\"]}]]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"2e:T3ac0,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia has the highest top marginal income tax rate in the country, and understanding how much you'll actually pay can feel overwhelming. But once you break it down into income tax, sales tax, and local rates, the picture becomes much clearer. Let's walk through everything you need to know about the tax amount in California for 2026.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"california-income-tax-rates-for-2026\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Income Tax Rates for 2026\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia uses a progressive income tax system with ten brackets, ranging from 1% to 13.3%. Unlike states with a flat tax, California's system means the more you earn, the higher your marginal rate — but only on the income within each bracket.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"2026-california-income-tax-brackets-single-filer\\\"\\u003e2026 California Income Tax Brackets (Single Filer)\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eTax Rate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eTaxable Income Range\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$0 – $10,756\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e2%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$10,757 – $25,499\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e4%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$25,500 – $40,245\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e6%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$40,246 – $55,866\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$55,867 – $70,606\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$70,607 – $360,659\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$360,660 – $432,787\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e11.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$432,788 – $721,314\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e12.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$721,315 – $1,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e13.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eOver $1,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe income tax rate you pay depends on where your income falls within these brackets. A single filer making $75,000 doesn't pay 9.3% on everything — they pay 1% on the first $10,756, 2% on the next portion, and so on. The result is an effective rate much lower than the top marginal rate.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse our \\u003ca href=\\\"/california-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eCalifornia tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to see your exact take-home pay after all California taxes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"understanding-district-taxes-in-california\\\"\\u003eUnderstanding District Taxes in California\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHere's something that catches a lot of people off guard: California has voter-approved \\u003cstrong\\u003edistrict taxes\\u003c/strong\\u003e that add to the state income tax rate. These are additional taxes passed by local voters to fund specific programs, and they vary by location.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAs of 2026, most California residents pay an additional 1% to 2% in district taxes on top of the state income tax rate. The rates range from 0% in some areas to over 3% in others. When you see the rates range across the state, it's because different districts have approved different measures.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you live in Los Angeles, San Francisco, or most major cities, expect to pay at least 1% extra in district taxes. This is separate from — and in addition to — the state income tax brackets shown above.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"california-sales-tax-rate-what-you-pay-at-the-register\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Sales Tax Rate: What You Pay at the Register\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia's state sales tax rate is 7.25%, which is already among the highest in the nation. But that's just the state portion. Local sales tax add-ons can push the total much higher.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"combined-sales-tax-rates-in-major-california-cities\\\"\\u003eCombined Sales Tax Rates in Major California Cities\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCity\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCombined Sales Tax Rate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eLos Angeles\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.5%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSan Francisco\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.625%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSan Diego\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7.75%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSan Jose\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.375%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSacramento\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.75%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe local sales tax varies by city and county because voters in each area can approve additional sales tax measures. This is why the sales tax rate you pay in one city can be significantly different from a neighboring city.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSome items are exempt from sales tax in California, including most groceries (unprepared food), prescription drugs, and certain clothing items. But restaurant meals, electronics, and most retail purchases are fully taxable.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"city-tax-and-additional-local-taxes\\\"\\u003eCity Tax and Additional Local Taxes\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eBeyond income tax and sales tax, some California cities impose additional taxes. San Francisco, for example, has several special taxes including a gross receipts tax for businesses and various parcel taxes for property owners. These city tax measures are typically voter-approved and fund specific municipal services.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you're a business owner in California, you'll also need to consider the state's $800 minimum franchise tax for LLCs and corporations, plus any local business license taxes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"how-to-calculate-your-total-tax-amount-in-california\\\"\\u003eHow to Calculate Your Total Tax Amount in California\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eTo figure out your total tax burden in California, you need to add up:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFederal income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e (based on federal brackets)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFICA taxes\\u003c/strong\\u003e (Social Security 6.2% + Medicare 1.45%)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCalifornia state income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e (1% to 13.3% based on brackets)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDistrict taxes\\u003c/strong\\u003e (0% to 3% depending on location)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSales tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e (7.25% to 10.5% depending on city, on taxable purchases)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eProperty tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e (approximately 0.71% average effective rate)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor a single person earning $75,000 in Los Angeles, the breakdown looks roughly like this:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eFederal income tax: ~$11,414\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eFICA: ~$5,738\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eCalifornia state income tax: ~$3,200\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eDistrict taxes: ~$750\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSales tax (estimated): ~$2,000\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal tax burden\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$23,102 (about 30.8% of gross income)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"california-tax-credits-you-should-know-about\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Tax Credits You Should Know About\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia offers several tax credits that can reduce your state tax bill:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCalifornia Earned Income Tax Credit\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Similar to the federal EITC, for low-to-moderate income workers\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eYoung Child Tax Credit\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Up to $1,083 for families with children under 6\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eRenter's Credit\\u003c/strong\\u003e: $60 (single) or $120 (married) for qualified renters\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCollege Access Tax Credit\\u003c/strong\\u003e: For contributions to California college funds\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThese credits are in addition to federal tax credits, and they can significantly reduce your overall tax burden.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"state-and-local-tax-deduction-cap\\\"\\u003eState and Local Tax Deduction Cap\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eRemember that the federal Tax Cuts and Jobs Act capped the state and local tax (SALT) deduction at $10,000. This means even if you pay $15,000 in California state income tax and property tax combined, you can only deduct $10,000 on your federal return. This cap hits California residents particularly hard because of the high state and local tax rates.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"comparing-california-to-other-states\\\"\\u003eComparing California to Other States\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia's total tax burden is among the highest in the nation. A single person earning $75,000 in California pays roughly $3,200 in state income tax alone, while the same person in Texas or Washington would pay $0 in state income tax. Use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/compare\\\"\\u003ecomparison tools\\u003c/a\\u003e to see how California stacks up against other states.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe trade-off, of course, is that California offers high wages, strong job markets, and extensive public services. But if you're considering a move, the tax savings from relocating to a no-income-tax state can be substantial — often $5,000 to $15,000+ per year.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"plan-your-california-taxes\\\"\\u003ePlan Your California Taxes\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse our \\u003ca href=\\\"/california-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Tax Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to get an accurate estimate of your take-home pay after all California taxes. The calculator factors in state income tax, district taxes, and FICA to give you the full picture.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"california-tax-rates-2025-vs-2026-what-changed\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Tax Rates 2025 vs 2026: What Changed?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you're comparing California tax rates 2025 with 2026, the brackets haven't changed structurally — California still uses the same ten-bracket progressive system. However, the income thresholds for each bracket are adjusted annually for inflation.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe California tax rate schedule 2026 reflects slightly higher bracket thresholds than 2025, meaning some filers will pay slightly less tax on the same income. The standard deduction and personal exemption also increased marginally.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"key-california-tax-rate-schedule-changes\\\"\\u003eKey California Tax Rate Schedule Changes\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor 2026, the California standard deduction is approximately $5,540 (single) and $11,080 (married filing jointly), up from $5,363 (single) and $10,726 (MFJ) in 2025.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you filed using California tax rates 2025 and want to compare, your 2026 bill should be very similar — possibly a few hundred dollars lower on the same income.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"los-angeles-sales-tax-and-san-francisco-income-tax\\\"\\u003eLos Angeles Sales Tax and San Francisco Income Tax\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia's local taxes vary significantly by city. Here's what you need to know about the two biggest markets:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"los-angeles-sales-tax\\\"\\u003eLos Angeles Sales Tax\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLos Angeles sales tax is currently 9.5% (combined state + county + local). This is one of the highest rates in California. If you're shopping in LA, expect to pay nearly 10% on most retail purchases. The Los Angeles sales tax funds county transportation, local schools, and city services.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"san-francisco-income-tax\\\"\\u003eSan Francisco Income Tax\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSan Francisco doesn't have a separate city income tax (unlike NYC). However, San Francisco income tax is effectively the California state income tax plus any district taxes that apply to SF residents. The combined state + district rate for San Francisco is approximately 1.5% additional on top of the state brackets.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSan Francisco also imposes a Gross Receipts Tax on businesses, which is separate from personal income tax. Use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/california-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eCalifornia tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to see your exact SF tax burden.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"california-sales-tax-on-cars-by-county-and-by-zip-code\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Sales Tax on Cars, by County, and by Zip Code\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"california-sales-tax-on-cars\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Sales Tax on Cars\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia sales tax on cars follows the same rate as general sales tax, based on where you register the vehicle (not where you buy it). So if you live in Los Angeles (9.5%), you pay 9.5% sales tax on a car purchase, even if you buy it in a lower-tax county. This can add thousands to your car purchase — on a $40,000 vehicle in LA, that's $3,800 in sales tax.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"california-sales-tax-by-county\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Sales Tax by County\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia sales tax by county ranges from 7.25% (base state rate in some rural areas) to 10.75% (in some Bay Area cities). The variation comes from voter-approved local measures. Here's a quick reference:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eLos Angeles County\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 9.5%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSan Francisco County\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 8.625%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSan Diego County\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 7.75%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eOrange County\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 7.75% - 8.25%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAlameda County\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 10.75% (highest)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"california-sales-tax-by-zip-code\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Sales Tax by Zip Code\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor exact California sales tax by zip code, the CDTFA (California Department of Tax and Fee Administration) provides an official lookup tool. Rates can vary even within the same city, so always verify your specific zip code. Our \\u003ca href=\\\"/sales-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003esales tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e handles this automatically.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"california-tax-calculator-for-1099-and-self-employed-workers\\\"\\u003eCalifornia Tax Calculator for 1099 and Self-Employed Workers\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you receive a 1099 or are self-employed in California, your tax situation is more complex. You'll pay:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFederal income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e (based on federal brackets)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSelf-employment tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e (15.3% on 92.35% of net income)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCalifornia state income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e (1% to 13.3% based on brackets)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCalifornia district taxes\\u003c/strong\\u003e (varies by location)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eA tax calculator California 1099 tool should factor in all four layers. Use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/self-employment-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eself-employment tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e for the federal + SE tax portion, then add California state tax using our \\u003ca href=\\\"/california-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eCalifornia tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor a $100,000 1099 income in California (single filer in Los Angeles):\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eFederal income tax: ~$14,000\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSelf-employment tax: ~$14,100\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eCalifornia state income tax: ~$6,800\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eDistrict taxes: ~$1,000\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e: ~$35,900 (about 35.9% of income)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"frequently-asked-questions-about-california-taxes\\\"\\u003eFrequently Asked Questions About California Taxes\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"how-much-is-100-000-income-taxed-in-california\\\"\\u003eHow much is $100,000 income taxed in California?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOn $100,000 income in California (single filer), you'll pay approximately $6,800 in state income tax, plus $14,000 in federal income tax, and $7,650 in FICA. Your total tax burden is about $28,450, leaving you with roughly $71,550 in take-home pay. Use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/california-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eCalifornia tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e for exact numbers.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"how-much-is-california-state-tax-on-200-000-income\\\"\\u003eHow much is California state tax on $200,000 income?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOn $200,000 income in California (single filer), state income tax alone is approximately $17,500. Add federal tax (~$42,000), FICA (~$10,500), and district taxes (~$2,000), and your total tax burden is roughly $72,000 — about 36% of your income.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"how-much-is-70-000-a-year-after-taxes-in-california\\\"\\u003eHow much is $70,000 a year after taxes in California?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOn $70,000 salary in California (single filer in LA), your take-home pay is approximately $50,500 after federal tax ($8,900), FICA ($5,355), state tax ($2,900), and district taxes ($350). That's an effective tax rate of about 27.8%.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"is-california-sales-tax-10\\\"\\u003eIs California sales tax 10%?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCalifornia sales tax can reach 10% or higher in some cities. The state base rate is 7.25%, but local add-ons push the combined rate to 10.75% in some areas (like Albany in Alameda County). Most major cities are between 8.5% and 9.75%.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"does-california-have-a-50-income-tax\\\"\\u003eDoes California have a 50% income tax?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eNo, California does not have a 50% income tax. The top marginal state rate is 13.3%, which applies to income over $1,000,000. However, when combined with the 37% federal top rate, the total marginal rate for very high earners can exceed 50% on the top dollars earned.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"are-california-taxes-high\\\"\\u003eAre California taxes high?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYes, California has the highest top marginal income tax rate in the nation (13.3%) and above-average sales tax rates. The overall tax burden in California is approximately 11.49% of income, ranking among the top 5 highest-tax states in the US.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"28:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-3xl lg:mx-0 lg:flex-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-container\\\",\\\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\\\":{\\\"__html\\\":\\\"$2e\\\"}}]}]\\n29:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"← Back to Blog\\\"}]}]\\n2a:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium bg-violet-100 text-violet-800 dark:bg-violet-500/20 dark:text-violet-400 border-violet-300 dark:border-violet-500/30\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"State Tax\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"June 19, 2026\\\"}]]}]\\n2b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"h3\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Washington State Tax Guide 2026: No Income Tax, But High Sales Tax\\\"}]\\n2c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Complete guide to Washington state taxes in 2026. No income tax, but sales tax up to 10.25%.\\\"}]\\n2d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"washington tax calculator\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"washington tax calculator\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"tax calculator washington\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"tax calculator washington\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"washington sales tax\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"washington sales tax\\\"}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1c:[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"charSet\\\":\\\"utf-8\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"viewport\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\"}]]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"2f:I[27201,[\\\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/61cc43b3d7ad2990.js\\\"],\\\"IconMark\\\"]\\n1a:null\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"California Tax Guide 2026: Income Tax, Sales Tax \\u0026 Local Rates | TheTaxCalc\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"description\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"Complete guide to California taxes in 2026. Income tax rates 1%-13.3%, sales tax rates by city, district taxes.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"link\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"rel\\\":\\\"author\\\",\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/about#rachel-mitchell\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"author\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"Rachel Mitchell, CPA\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"keywords\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"california tax calculator,tax amount in california,sales tax rate,local sales tax,income tax rate,district taxes\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"creator\\\",\\\"content\\\":